  "instructions": "This is Olumi's own check of a first draft; the user has not asked for it. Anything you add is shown as Olumi's suggestion for the user to keep or remove. Where the text below says the user asked or will approve, read it as: Olumi is suggesting, and the user decides.\nFor each risk, set restates_id to the id of a limit or risk below that it only restates, or null when it adds something new. Limits the user already set: []. Risks already in the model: [].\nMETHOD TURN: the user asked Olumi to suggest risks they have not considered. Do not write prose. Reply with ONLY <risk_suggestions>JSON array</risk_suggestions>, nothing before or after it. The server writes every word the user sees.\nMETHOD: assumption-based planning. For each option below, find what it RELIES ON in the model (a factor it changes) and how that could fail. Use the prompt list (people, timing, cost, dependency, external) for coverage, and keep up to 3 risks, each from a DIFFERENT class. Risks on what the options DIFFER on come first; a risk on a factor no option changes affects every option alike, so offer it only if there is room. Ground each in the brief, the conversation or the model.\nEach item: {\"label\": a new risk name, 6 words or fewer, no figures; \"category\": one of people|timing|cost|dependency|external; \"hits_id\": the id of the option it hits (or, for an assumption every option shares, the id of a factor NO option changes); \"through_id\": the id of a factor which that option CHANGES (listed under it), or the same factor id when hits_id is a factor; \"mechanism\": \"drives\" when more or less of the through factor itself makes the risk more likely or severe, \"relies_on\" when the option relies on something (a precondition or timing dependency) that could fail. Timing and dependency items always use \"relies_on\"; \"through_direction\": \"positive\" when more of that factor makes the risk more likely or severe, else \"negative\" (used only for \"drives\"); \"affects_id\": the id of the goal or an outcome the risk would hurt; \"direction\": \"negative\" when the risk lowers it, \"positive\" when it raises it; \"relies_on\": what the option relies on, 12 words or fewer, starting with a verb or noun (e.g. \"filling both roles quickly\"); \"watch_for\": the early sign, 8 words or fewer}.\nA \"relies_on\" risk must name one option: shared preconditions cannot be applied yet and will be refused. It stays on the model with no links and is left out of the Run until the model can apply it to that option alone.\nThe goal: {\"id\":\"monthly_recurring_revenue\",\"label\":\"monthly recurring revenue\"}.\nGoal and outcomes (affects_id): [{\"id\":\"monthly_recurring_revenue\",\"label\":\"monthly recurring revenue\"},{\"id\":\"starter_tier_mrr\",\"label\":\"Starter-tier MRR\"}].\nThe user's options and the factors each one changes: [{\"id\":\"launch_49_starter_tier\",\"label\":\"Launch \u00a349 starter tier\",\"changes\":[{\"id\":\"starter_tier_available\",\"label\":\"Starter tier available\"},{\"id\":\"starter_tier_price\",\"label\":\"Starter-tier price\"}]},{\"id\":\"raise_prices_10\",\"label\":\"Raise prices 10%\",\"changes\":[{\"id\":\"existing_customer_price_change\",\"label\":\"Existing-customer price change\"}]}].\nAll factors: [{\"id\":\"existing_customer_price_change\",\"label\":\"Existing-customer price change\"},{\"id\":\"customers_lost_from_price_rise\",\"label\":\"Customers lost from price rise\"},{\"id\":\"starter_tier_available\",\"label\":\"Starter tier available\"},{\"id\":\"new_starter_subscribers\",\"label\":\"New starter subscribers\"},{\"id\":\"starter_tier_price\",\"label\":\"Starter-tier price\"}].\nUse only these ids. Never write a probability, percentage, figure, likely, chance, best, recommend, winner, leads or ahead.",
